New `src/shared/telemetry/for-repo.ts` (`#shared/*` imports only):

export interface RepoTelemetryInput {
  readonly config: AppConfig;            // machine-level: consent in config.telemetry.enabled
  readonly repo: RepoConfig;
  readonly registered: boolean;
  readonly sessionMode: SessionMode;
  readonly serverVersion: string;
  readonly graphSchemaVersion: number;
  readonly now?: () => Date;
}
export function telemetryForRepo(input: RepoTelemetryInput): Telemetry;

Returns `nullTelemetry()` when `recordingEnabled({ consent: input.config.telemetry.enabled, repo: input.repo, registered: input.registered })` is false, and otherwise `createTelemetry({ dir: join(input.repo.dataDir, "telemetry"), repoRoot: input.repo.repoRoot, dataDir: input.repo.dataDir, sessionMode, serverVersion, graphSchemaVersion, now })`. No environment read, no second consent rule: the precedence itself is `recordingEnabled` from `#shared/repo-config` (TASK-003), which this function must not re-implement. Do not change `src/shared/telemetry/telemetry.ts` or `record.ts`.

`src/shared/telemetry/html.ts` gains exactly one export and keeps `renderHtml` byte-identical (its existing test asserts the single-repo output):

/** The panels of one report, without <html>/<head>: the multi-repo page reuses them. */
export function renderBody(data: ReportData): string;

`renderHtml(data, meta)` becomes `head + renderBody(data)` so no panel markup is duplicated.

New `src/shared/telemetry/html-multi.ts`:

export interface ReportSectionView {
  readonly label: string;      // the repo's basename plus its canonical path, already escaped by the renderer
  readonly sourceDir: string;  // the telemetry directory this record set was read from
  readonly data: ReportData;
}
export function renderMultiReport(input: {
  readonly machine: ReportData;
  readonly sections: readonly ReportSectionView[];
  readonly generated_at: string;
  readonly since: string | null;
}): string;

One `<!doctype html>` document: one `<style>` block (reuse the existing stylesheet text, do not add a second source of styles), a machine-total section first, one `<a href="#repo-N">` per section in a `<nav>`, then one `<section id="repo-N">` per input section with its label, its escaped `sourceDir` and `renderBody(section.data)`. Every interpolated value goes through `escapeHtml`. No external asset: the output must not contain `http://`, `https://`, `src=`, `<link`, `@import` or `url(`. An empty registry renders the machine section with a "no registered repository has recorded anything yet" line and still no external asset.

New `src/shared/telemetry/serve.ts`:

export const DEFAULT_REPORT_PORT = 7317;
export const LOOPBACK_HOSTS = ["127.0.0.1", "::1", "localhost"] as const;
export interface ServeOptions {
  readonly home: string;
  readonly port?: number;
  readonly host?: string;
  readonly since?: string | undefined;   // YYYY-MM-DD or Nd, as the verb accepts
  readonly now?: () => Date;
  readonly log?: Logger;
}
export interface ReportServer { readonly url: string; readonly port: number; readonly host: string; readonly repositories: number; close(): Promise<void> }
export interface ReportSections { readonly machine: ReportData; readonly sections: readonly ReportSectionView[] }
export function reportSections(home: string, since: string | null, now?: () => Date): ReportSections;
export async function serveReport(options: ServeOptions): Promise<ReportServer>;

`reportSections` reads `join(home, "sangkar.json")` with `readRegistry`, and for each registered root reads `join(root, ".sangkar", "telemetry")` with `readRecords`/`aggregate` into one section labelled `<basename> — <root>`; a repo with no telemetry directory still yields a section with zero records; `machine` is `aggregate` over the union of all records with the summed `unparsed`. `serveReport` refuses a host outside `LOOPBACK_HOSTS` by throwing `new CliUsageError(..., ...)` from `#shared/cli/args` **before** binding, defaults to `127.0.0.1` and `DEFAULT_REPORT_PORT`, and returns a server with `Bun.serve` whose `repositories` is the registry's repo count at bind time. Routes, recomputed per request so a long-lived server shows new records: `GET /` → 200 `text/html` from `renderMultiReport`; `GET /healthz` → 200 `text/plain` `ok\n`; `HEAD /` → 200 with no body; anything else → 404. `close()` resolves when the listener has stopped and is idempotent.

New `src/shared/pidlock.ts`, a sibling of `store/lock.ts` (which is not edited) implementing the same create-exclusive + stale-reclaim pattern for a pid file:

export const PID_STALE_AFTER_MS = 60_000;
export interface PidLockHandle { readonly path: string; readonly pid: number; readonly startedAt: string }
export interface PidLockState { readonly held: boolean; readonly pid: number | null; readonly stale: boolean; readonly startedAt: string | null }
export async function acquirePidLock(path: string): Promise<PidLockHandle>;   // AppError("index_locked") when a live pid holds it
export async function readPidLock(path: string): Promise<PidLockState>;
export async function releasePidLock(handle: PidLockHandle): Promise<void>;   // idempotent: a missing file is success
export async function heartbeatPidLock(handle: PidLockHandle): Promise<void>;

Content is `{"pid":<n>,"startedAt":"<ISO>"}` written with `flag: "wx"`; a lock is stale when its pid is gone (`process.kill(pid, 0)` with `ESRCH`) or its mtime is older than `PID_STALE_AFTER_MS`; an unparseable file is reclaimed by age alone; reclaim is `unlink` then one retry.

New `src/daemon/lifecycle.ts` (may import `#shared/*` and `src/indexer/**`; never `src/tools/**`):

export interface DaemonRunOptions { readonly home: string; readonly onTick: () => Promise<void>; readonly intervalMs?: number; readonly log?: Logger; readonly signal?: AbortSignal; readonly lock?: PidLockHandle }
export async function runDaemon(options: DaemonRunOptions): Promise<number>;
async function startDaemon(home: string): Promise<number>;
export async function stopDaemon(home: string): Promise<number>;
export async function daemonStatus(home: string): Promise<DaemonStatus>;

**Lock ownership:** `runDaemon` uses `options.lock` when given and otherwise acquires `join(home, "daemon.pid")` itself, and it **never releases a caller-supplied lock** — the acquirer releases it, so one handle is never released twice. It calls `onTick()` once immediately, then loops on an `unref`'d interval (`DEFAULT_DAEMON_TICK_MS = 5_000`, exported) and heartbeats every tick. **A throwing first `onTick` releases a lock this function acquired and propagates** (a daemon that cannot serve leaves no pid file). `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM` or the injected `signal` stop the loop, stop the heartbeat, release only a self-acquired lock and resolve 0. `startDaemon` spawns a detached `bin/sangkar.ts daemon run` with stdout+stderr appended to `<home>/daemon.log`, waits <= 5 s for `daemon.pid`, returns 0 or 1; `stopDaemon` SIGTERMs the recorded pid, waits <= 5 s for the file to go, returns 0, and 1 when nothing was running; `daemonStatus` builds `DaemonStatus` from the pid file and one `RepoRuntime` per registry entry via `readRepoConfig`. `startDaemon` resolves the CLI entry the way `src/tools/index/handler.ts:workerInvocation` does.

New `src/daemon/reconcile.ts` (imports `#shared/*`, `src/indexer/watch.ts`, `src/daemon/types.ts`; never `src/tools/**`):

export const RECONCILE_INTERVAL_MS = 5_000;   // the daemon's default tick, consumed by TASK-018

export interface WatcherOptions { readonly repoRoot: string; readonly dataDir: string; readonly intervalMs: number; readonly spawn?: SpawnWorker; readonly onSpawn?: (event: unknown) => void }
export interface ReconcileOptions {
  readonly home: string;
  readonly spawn?: SpawnWorker;                                        // startWatch's own seam
  readonly openWatcher?: (options: WatcherOptions) => WatchHandle;      // defaults to `startWatch`
  readonly now?: () => number;
  readonly log?: Logger;
}
export interface ReconcileReport { readonly watched: readonly string[]; readonly started: readonly string[]; readonly stopped: readonly string[]; readonly missing: readonly string[]; readonly skipped: readonly string[] }
export interface ReconcileHandle { tick(): Promise<ReconcileReport>; stats(): ReadonlyMap<string, WatchStats>; stop(): Promise<void> }
export function startReconcile(options: ReconcileOptions): ReconcileHandle;

`tick()` reads the registry with `readRegistry(join(home, "sangkar.json"))`, then per entry reads `readRepoConfig(root, { dataDir: join(root, ".sangkar") })`: a root whose directory is missing → `missing`, no watcher, one deduped log line; `watch === false` → `skipped` and any watcher for it stopped; `watch === true` → exactly one watcher through `openWatcher({ repoRoot: root, dataDir: join(root, ".sangkar"), intervalMs: watchIntervalMs, spawn })`, kept in a `Map<root, { handle, intervalMs }>`; a changed `watchIntervalMs` stops and restarts that one watcher and reports it in both arrays; a root no longer registered is stopped and forgotten.

**`startReconcile` starts no timer.** It returns a passive handle; the daemon's own loop (`runDaemon`) calls `tick()`, so there is exactly one scheduler in the process. `stop()` stops every watcher and makes a later `tick()` a no-op. The module never spawns a worker itself: `backoffMs` and the spawn stay inside `watch.ts`.

New `src/daemon/main.ts`, the only module that composes the three daemon slices:

export interface ForegroundOptions {
  readonly home: string;
  readonly port?: number;
  readonly host?: string;
  readonly spawn?: SpawnWorker;
  readonly openWatcher?: (options: WatcherOptions) => WatchHandle;   // passthrough to reconcile
  readonly intervalMs?: number;                                       // daemon tick, default RECONCILE_INTERVAL_MS
  readonly onTick?: () => Promise<void>;                              // test seam, called after each reconcile tick
  readonly now?: () => Date;
  readonly signal?: AbortSignal;
}
export async function runDaemonForeground(options: ForegroundOptions): Promise<number>;

Composition, in this order: `const lock = await acquirePidLock(join(home, "daemon.pid"))` → `startReconcile({ home, spawn, openWatcher })` → `let server: ReportServer | null = null; server = await serveReport({ home, port, host, now })` → `log.info("daemon.ready", { home, url: server.url, port: server.port })` → `await runDaemon({ home, lock, intervalMs: options.intervalMs ?? RECONCILE_INTERVAL_MS, log, signal, onTick: async () => { const report = await reconcile.tick(); await options.onTick?.(); log.info("daemon.tick", { watched: report.watched.length, missing: report.missing.length }); } })` → in a `finally`: `await reconcile.stop()`, `await server?.close()`, `await releasePidLock(lock)`. `server` starts as `null` and is closed with `?.` so a bind failure still reaches the release. `releasePidLock` is idempotent and `runDaemon` never releases a caller-supplied lock (TASK-016), so the handle is released exactly once, here.

Because the lock is acquired first, a second `runDaemonForeground` on one home rejects with `index_locked` with nothing bound and no watcher started; because the release is last and unconditional, a bind failure (port in use) leaves no pid file. No MCP, no database, no `store/**` import.
